<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.16.1: Three Forms of Quadratic Expressions</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02026</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.16.1: Three Forms of Quadratic Expressions</md:title>
    <md:uuid>1c677028-d6db-4570-a042-4717aaf6b0af</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section class="practice" id="sect-00001"><title>Warm Up Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00001">Expressions in different forms can be used to define the same function.
        Here are three ways to define a function <m:math display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mi>f</m:mi>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math>.</para>
      <table class="unnumbered os-raise-wideequaltable" id="table-00001">
        <tgroup cols="3">
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <entry><emphasis>Standard form</emphasis></entry>
              <entry><emphasis>Factored form</emphasis></entry>
              <entry><emphasis>Vertex form</emphasis></entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry>
                <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                    <m:msup>
                      <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                      <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                    </m:msup>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>4</m:mn>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>
              </entry>
              <entry>
                <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>3</m:mn>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>
              </entry>
              <entry>
                <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                    <m:msup>
                      <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                      <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                    </m:msup>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>
              </entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry>
                <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>a</m:mi>
                    <m:msup>
                      <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                      <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                    </m:msup>
                    <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>b</m:mi>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>c</m:mi>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>
              </entry>
              <entry>
                <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>a</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:msub>
                      <m:mi>r</m:mi>
                      <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                    </m:msub>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:msub>
                      <m:mi>r</m:mi>
                      <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                    </m:msub>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>
              </entry>
              <entry>
                <m:math display="inline">
                  <m:mrow>
                    <m:mi>f</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                    <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>a</m:mi>
                    <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                    <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>h</m:mi>
                    <m:msup>
                      <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                      <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                    </m:msup>
                    <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                    <m:mi>k</m:mi>
                  </m:mrow>
                </m:math>
              </entry>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </tgroup>
      </table>

      <para id="para-00002">Which form would you use if you want to find the following features of
        the graph of <m:math display="inline">
          <m:mrow>
            <m:mi>f</m:mi>
          </m:mrow>
        </m:math>? Be prepared to explain your
        reasoning.</para>

        <exercise id="_9cf689e7-5f8c-4a48-ba74-2a2f83303671" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00001">
            <para id="para-00003">The <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>-intercepts</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00001">
            <para id="para-00004">Correct! Factored form. In factored form, we can see that the <m:math
                display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>-intercepts, or
              roots, are located at <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:msub>
                    <m:mi>r</m:mi>
                    <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                  </m:msub>
                  <m:mo>,</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>0</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>
              and <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:msub>
                    <m:mi>r</m:mi>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  </m:msub>
                  <m:mo>,</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>0</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>.</para>
            <para id="para-00005">Incorrect. The correct answer is factored form. In factored form,
              we can see that the <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mi>x</m:mi>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>-intercepts, or
              roots, are located at <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:msub>
                    <m:mi>r</m:mi>
                    <m:mn>1</m:mn>
                  </m:msub>
                  <m:mo>,</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>0</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>
              and <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:msub>
                    <m:mi>r</m:mi>
                    <m:mn>2</m:mn>
                  </m:msub>
                  <m:mo>,</m:mo>
                  <m:mn>0</m:mn>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise id="_380e1643-a11c-4cb6-8845-ec65a4da1a50" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00002">
            <para id="para-00006">The vertex</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00002">
            <para id="para-00007">Correct! Vertex form. In vertex form, we can see that the graph
              has a vertex located at the point defined by <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mi>h</m:mi>
                  <m:mo>,</m:mo>
                  <m:mi>k</m:mi>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>.</para>
            <para id="para-00008">Incorrect. The correct answer is vertex form. In vertex form, we
              can see that the graph has a vertex located at the point defined by <m:math
                display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                  <m:mi>h</m:mi>
                  <m:mo>,</m:mo>
                  <m:mi>k</m:mi>
                  <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise id="_6e893041-f82f-43ab-a110-2237251bfb5d" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00003">
            <para id="para-00009">The <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>-intercept</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00003">
            <para id="para-00010">Correct! In standard form, we can see that the graph intersects
              the <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>-axis at 3, but in
              any form, we can pretty easily find the <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>-intercept when the
              input is 0.</para>
            <para id="para-00011">Incorrect. The correct answer is standard form. In standard form,
              we can see that the graph intersects the <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>-axis at 3, but in
              any form, we can pretty easily find the <m:math display="inline">
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mi>y</m:mi>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:math>-intercept when the
              input is 0.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
    </section>
  </content>
</document>